WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.023 --> 00:00:07.940
MARIANNE SLIGHT: Welcome
to our session today,

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.240
accelerating insights with
the health analytics platform.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.300
I'm Marianne Slight, a product
manager on the health care

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.890
team of Google Cloud.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.200
And with me is my
colleague, Vivian Neilley,

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.480
who leads the health
care customer engineering

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.590
specialists.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.170
We're going to talk
today about clinical data

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.320
and the challenges facing
health systems as they deal

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.200
with a proliferation
of the HR instances

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.760
and ancillary clinical systems.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.920
So how do you get your data
into the new standard spire

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.770
or into the OMOP
common data model?

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.960
How do you align your
different modalities

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.570
of data, your different
terminologies of data?

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.810
How do you create and
manage the pipelines

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.750
to handle that data
transformation?

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.960
How do you abstract
out structured data

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.780
from the unstructured
clinical mode?

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.130
And then how do you analyze
this data in a coherent fashion

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.722
without needing
advanced SQL skills?

00:01:04.722 --> 00:01:06.180
These are the
challenges that we're

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.550
going to talk about
within this presentation.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.170
So let's start off by talking
about data harmonization--

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:19.090
how you harmonize all of your
different siloed data sources

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:23.650
into something that's coherent
and ready for analysis.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.340
And typically, you might
have several different EHR

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.520
instances, each of which
is configured differently

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.660
from the next.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.030
Or perhaps you've
acquired hospitals,

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:38.490
and they use a different
EHR system than you did.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:41.670
As well as the EHR
data sources, you also

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.720
have clinical ancillary systems
like lab systems or pharmacy

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:46.890
systems.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.120
And all of this data harmonized
into something coherent

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.880
enables you to
analyze more richly

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.430
your clinical environment.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.460
But it's not just current
data that's important.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.600
You also need to add
on the historical data.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.750
And so what we typically see
are historical data sources

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:12.180
that come to Google Cloud in the
form of CSV files or databases

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.210
with incremental or
streaming data coming in

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:20.720
through HR7 version 2 or
using the new FHIR standard.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.640
And that's dealing
with the data sources.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.390
What you harmonize to is
over on the right hand side--

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.230
some kind target schema.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:33.930
And that might be something
like the FHIR schema,

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.990
or it might be something
like the OMOP observation

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.710
or common data model that
is used for analyzing data

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.660
for research purposes.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.930
But most commonly,
it's some combination

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:50.250
of a standard schema and
then custom extensions

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.770
in order to support any
investments you've already

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:57.210
made for applications
that sit on top of it.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.520
And so supporting
custom schemas is also

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.910
part of our data
harmonization suite.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.850
Now, in order to go
from your data sources

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.790
into your target
schema, you typically

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.420
need to have your data undergo
four types of transformation.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.860
The first of these is field
mappings, where perhaps you're

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:21.690
taking patient_ID in
this database over here,

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.720
you've got MRN, or
medical record number,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.840
as the name of the field
over in this data source,

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.780
and perhaps you've also got
member ID in another data

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.720
source, and you're
trying to harmonize it

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:37.860
into, say, the FHIR schema
with the patient resource

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.830
and the identifier attribute.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.300
That's field mappings.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.030
Terminology mappings
looks at how

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:49.090
you label the clinical
concepts within your data.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:51.750
So perhaps you're
capturing your medications

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.280
using first databank in this
EHR, but over in Sterner,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.890
you're capturing them using
the [? Moulton ?] medical

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:00.150
terminology.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.930
And perhaps you want to
harmonize those into RX norm.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.990
These are three different
clinical terminologies

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.240
that are used for
medications, and you

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.030
need to be able to
harmonize the concepts

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:16.440
into a coherent single
clinical terminology for each

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.130
of your different concept types.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:23.400
Units alignment is another
step within data harmonization

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.480
because different lab systems
produce the lab test results

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.040
with different units.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.590
Often that's just by
a factor of 1,000,

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:34.440
but sometimes it's a little
more broader than that.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.730
As when a particular lab test,
its standard unit of measure

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.830
changes at a particular
point in time.

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:43.650
And in that case,
the system needs

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.760
to understand the point
at which the units changed

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.940
from one to another in
order that you can end up

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.970
with harmonized data
in consistent units

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.430
in your analytic target.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.940
And finally, abstracting
out structured data

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.400
from the unstructured
clinical note.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.330
And I'll talk more
about that in a moment.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.560
So we recently launched our
mapping configuration language,

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.950
which we call Whistle
And we've launched

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.040
this to open source so that
the entire community can

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.240
benefit from this.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.620
What the Whistle mapping
configuration language does

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:26.660
is it enables you to convert
from an input in JSON format

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:30.830
into an output in JSON
format using a mapping

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:34.260
configuration that you've
written using Whistle.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.440
And what's unique
about Whistle is

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.800
that it supports repeated
and nested structures, which,

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.295
of course, are so critical
within support for HR 7

00:05:43.295 --> 00:05:47.690
version 2 and within support
for the FHIR standard.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:52.280
It also supports calls for
terminology maps, calls

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.150
for unit harmonization, and
for clinical entity extraction

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:58.610
using natural
language processing.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:04.080
And you can create your own
user defined functions, as well.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.270
Now, here's an example of
how you might convert an HR 7

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.460
message that
incorporates the name

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:15.130
and address of a particular
health system into FHIR.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.840
And the gray box at
the bottom, that's

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.430
an example of the
Whistle mapping

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:23.510
configuration that you
would create to do that.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.870
When we launched our Whistle
data transformation language

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.690
within our open source
GitHub repository,

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:33.270
we also launched sample
maps to get you started.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.100
We included sample maps for two
different message types of HR 7

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:42.110
version 2 map to FHIR, and
we also included sample maps

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:44.620
for mapping from FHIR to OMOP.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:48.950
And these are all available
within our GitHub repo.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:51.620
So you've created this
Whistle mapping configuration.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.420
Now how do you run it?

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:55.310
We have three
different ways that you

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.920
can run a Whistle
mapping configuration,

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.130
create a pipeline,
and convert your data.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:06.330
The first is using the
Google Cloud health care API.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:10.250
So if you're importing your
data from cloud storage

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:14.240
into the FHIR API, or if
you're exporting your data

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:19.100
from the FHIR API into
BigQuery, or if you're

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.680
moving your data from
the HR 7 version 2

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.340
store into the FHIR
store on Google Cloud,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.330
in each of these cases you can
incorporate a Whistle mapping

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:35.520
configuration to transform the
data as you move it across.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.760
The second way is using
Cloud Data Fusion,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.530
which is a Google tool that is
a fully managed service for ETL

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:44.970
pipelines.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.400
And what that allows
you to do is have access

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.420
to a library of different
pre-configured sources,

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.640
and connectors, and
transformations,

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:58.450
and a library of reconfigured
outputs or sinks, as well.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:00.780
And what's really unique
about Cloud Data Fusion

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:03.540
is that this is a fully
managed environment

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.770
that does it by drag and
drop using a graphical UI.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.350
And that helps you to
get away from managing

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:13.470
all of your pipelines
with code into focusing

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:16.230
on the actual transformations
that are getting done

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:21.230
and moving you away
towards insight and action.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:24.770
The third way is an open
sourced cloud data flow

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:29.570
pipeline that we launched
earlier that converts--

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.049
this reference pipeline
converts streaming HR 7 version

00:08:33.049 --> 00:08:36.159
2 messages into FHIR resources.

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:39.270
And so that, too, is
available on our GitHub repo.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:43.710
Now, I want to talk
about clinical notes

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:45.670
briefly in a bit more detail.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:47.910
This is an example
of an ICU note.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:48.960
It's very dense.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.540
This is just a
very small section

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.520
of what's typically
reams of paper

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:55.170
if you were to print it all out.

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.390
It's reams of paper
because a lot of it

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.670
is copied from one
node to another.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.410
It's copied forward.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.840
But the other issue with
these kinds of notes

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.730
is it's difficult to find the
real wealth of information

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:12.010
that's buried in
this density of text.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.790
And it's dense because there's
a lot of different types

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:16.620
of abbreviation.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:20.310
It's also dense with a lot
of different weirdities

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.580
or oddities in punctuation,
because some of this note

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:26.640
is actually
generated by the EHR.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:28.710
And then it's not
just speaking English,

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:32.130
it's also speaking
Latin, as well.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:34.430
And what's challenging
about the clinical note

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:37.970
is when you look at it, each
of these different roles

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:40.560
might see something different.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.350
So when a physician looks
at that note and says, well,

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:45.740
does this patient have sepsis?

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.770
They will be looking at it
from a different perspective

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:51.240
than the medical coder,
than the CDI nurse,

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.720
than the clinical
quality analyst.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:56.300
The physician is typically
looking at it to say,

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:59.240
could this patient
possibly have sepsis?

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:02.360
Because if so, he or she
is going to do something

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:03.860
different as a
result. They're going

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.660
to follow a different
care pathway.

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:10.200
Whereas the medical coder is
looking at the note to say,

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:14.180
does this note justify
a diagnosis of sepsis?

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:17.060
Because if so, that will
result in a different code

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:19.010
on the medical claim.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:22.070
They undergo different training,
and these different roles

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:25.730
are looking at it one from
the perspective of discovery,

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:29.100
the other from the
perspective of compliance.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.840
So there are different models
for these different use cases,

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:36.520
and clearly one size
does not fit all.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:39.920
And so what we are looking at
in terms of clinical entity

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.720
extraction are these four areas.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.680
The first is abstracting
the actual clinical concepts

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.640
themselves-- the diagnoses, the
medications, the procedures,

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:52.050
the observations, and so on.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:55.280
The second is looking at the
relations and the attributes

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.860
about those key
clinical entities.

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:01.460
So for example, with
medications, what was the dose?

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:02.850
What was the route?

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.180
When was it ordered?

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:09.080
Functional features talks
about the semantic context

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:10.820
of the clinical entities.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:14.910
So was that diagnosis actually
positive for the patient,

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:18.770
or was it documented as
a rule out diagnosis?

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:22.430
Did this other diagnosis
impact the patient,

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:25.820
or instead did it pertain
to the patient's parent,

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:30.190
and actually it should be part
of family and medical history?

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:34.510
Terminology mappings, because
when you abstract out this data

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:38.180
it needs to be coded into one
of those clinical terminologies

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:44.030
that the rest of the
structured data described as.

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:46.890
I said that one model
doesn't fit all,

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:51.410
and so we also enable tuning
of these clinical entity

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:54.530
extraction models using AutoML.

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:58.790
And AutoML allows you to
augment or enrich a model

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:01.610
with a sample of your
own data that you

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:05.360
have labeled for your unique
documentation practices.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:08.720
Or perhaps you have
unique abbreviations.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:13.030
Perhaps you've got specific use
cases that you need to support,

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:16.100
and AutoML allows you
to tune that model

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:18.110
for your specific circumstance.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:21.590
So you've harmonized your data
using our data harmonization

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:23.810
tools into something coherent.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:26.360
Now how do you analyze it?

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:31.250
Leveraging BigQuery enables us
to provide scalable analytics.

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:35.240
It's analytic database, because
it's structured as a columnar

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:38.270
database, and it's
scalable because it scales

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:41.480
through petabytes horizontally.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.900
All of the data is encrypted
both at rest and in motion

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:49.700
by default, and the data
is accessible using SQL.

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:52.760
So all of those business
intelligence tools over

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:56.450
on the right hand side
can access the data

00:12:56.450 --> 00:13:01.230
within BigQuery using
their native capabilities.

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:05.780
We also have a number of public
data sets available on BigQuery

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:10.980
for you to integrate into
your analysis, as well.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:14.950
And we recently launched
the OMOP common data model

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:19.840
as an open source GitHub
repo on top of BigQuery.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.560
And further, the ATLAS
cohort definition tool

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:27.910
on top of Cloud Marketplace
in Google Cloud.

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:29.440
And what that
allows you to do is

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:32.410
to use the OMOP
common data model

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:36.290
and analyze the data
on BigQuery directly.

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:39.020
So understanding
the terminologies,

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.550
the vocabularies, looking at
database-level statistics,

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:44.950
understanding your
patient profiles,

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:47.710
and looking at your
cohort definitions

00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:52.720
using the ATLAS tool all
directly on top of BigQuery.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:56.740
This is an example of
the ATLAS cohort manager

00:13:56.740 --> 00:14:01.540
tool looking at the prevalence
of medications in this data set

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:04.240
and understanding the
different medications

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:07.860
and their prevalence.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.020
I said we had a number of
public data sets available.

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:13.500
This is a subset of the
health care data sets

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:15.780
that are available on BigQuery.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:18.880
The first two are large
synthetic data sets

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:21.210
in two different
forms to allow you

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:24.880
to experiment with these
different types of data.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:28.800
The first is FHIR using the
synthetic data from MITRE,

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:33.060
and then second is [? Synpath ?]
using synthetic data

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:35.760
in the OMOP common data model.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:39.390
But as well as those
two synthetic data sets,

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:43.710
we have a large number of
curated current data sets.

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:46.290
Most recently we
have added data sets

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:49.600
in the fields of social
determinants of health,

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:52.470
and these are kept
current every quarter

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:57.900
and take social determinant data
such as incope data, snap data,

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:02.490
homelessness down to, in
many cases, the county level

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:06.840
so that you can match this data
with your own health system

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:10.890
data in order to understand the
impact of social determinants

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:13.570
on your particular population.

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:18.330
We also added the COVID-19 case
data from the New York Times,

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:20.870
and then there are a
number of other data sets--

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:22.410
health care data sets--

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.690
that are available
and kept current.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:28.700
And we're adding
more all the time.

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:31.880
Finally, how do
you analyze data?

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:35.300
You analyze data
using dashboards often

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:37.590
in order to generate action.

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:39.140
So what are the
decisions that you

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:43.160
want to make on the
basis of that analysis?

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:47.270
Well dashboards, and in
particular our Looker offering,

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:50.120
enables you to look at
key performance indicators

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:52.050
from your health system.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:55.730
In this example, I'm looking at
readmission rates, mortality,

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:59.390
and average length of stay
across my entire hospital

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:00.650
system.

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:03.050
Obviously, I can look
at that over time.

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:05.990
I can drill into
specific facilities.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:08.210
Over on the right
hand side, you can

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:11.240
see that I'm looking at
some of the key diagnoses

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:14.660
and procedures those
costly, chronic, often

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:18.890
comorbid conditions,
and looking at where

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:23.000
I have a potential to
improve those key performance

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.420
indicators.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:27.150
And I can drill into
that in more detail.

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:28.770
And if I drill
into, for example,

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:33.950
AMI, then I can see for
my AMI patients, those

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:38.870
on the right hand side who are
most at risk of bouncing back

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:40.740
with a readmission.

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:45.350
And that risk score has been
generated using a machine

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:50.420
learning model directly within
BigQuery using a BQML model

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:54.440
that takes advantage of some
of the public data sets that we

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:58.580
looked at earlier-- some of the
social determinant data sets--

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:01.010
but also takes
into account things

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:04.730
like the number of comorbidities
and the discharge date

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:09.300
relative to now, as well
as the age of the patient.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:14.060
And so finally, these are
some of the demonstration

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:18.230
of the correlations
between the risk score

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:21.290
and some of the social
determinant data.

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:28.265
In these examples, household
data and SNAP enrollment data.

00:17:28.265 --> 00:17:30.640
So we've talked about the
capabilities of the health care

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:31.880
analytics platform.

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:34.090
Let's talk about one
specific use case, and that's

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:36.430
a clinical data cloud for FHIR.

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:38.080
Because increasingly,
we're seeing

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:44.500
FHIR in use in health systems
as its central real time

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:47.650
repository for
clinical data because

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:51.720
of its longitudinal
patient records support.

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:55.420
And so in this example here,
you harmonize your data

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:58.180
into FHIR using the
tools that we've talked

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:00.520
about for data harmonization.

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:04.240
You make the data available
in analytics on BigQuery,

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:07.930
you store the data natively
in the health care FHIR store

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:10.150
as part of the health care API.

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:13.800
All of this sits on top of the
data protection suite, which

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:17.270
encompass our support
for HIPAA best practices,

00:18:17.270 --> 00:18:20.380
and we also enable
access to that data

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:23.560
through the FHIR application
layer that includes

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:27.337
SMART on FHIR and also Apigee.

00:18:27.337 --> 00:18:29.420
And now I'm going to hand
it over to my colleague,

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:34.420
Vivian Neilley, who's going to
talk about the architecture.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:36.070
VIVIAN NEILLEY:
Thank you, Marianne.

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:40.080
So when we think through how
data flows through this system,

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:45.450
there are five conceptual steps
it takes to get to a place

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:48.630
where users can of service it
to applications or to perhaps

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.540
to other analytics use cases.

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:54.090
As Marianne discussed, there are
a vast amount of data sources

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:55.590
that can be ingested.

00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:58.350
From EHR data, to
patient generated

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:01.760
data, even with imaging data.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:03.910
All of these different
source systems

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:06.190
result in different
products being

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:08.620
utilized for data ingestion.

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:11.410
Our health care
API, for example,

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:14.680
takes three different
modalities, FHIR, DICOM,

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:16.240
and HL7.

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:20.710
While something like our cloud
storage or blob storage product

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:23.860
can handle a wide
variety of data

00:19:23.860 --> 00:19:27.790
that may be coming from
data warehouses, or patient,

00:19:27.790 --> 00:19:33.140
or other data sources truly,
the data harmonization layer

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:35.360
is really where
the magic happens.

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:39.240
All the aspects of harmonization
are handled in this step,

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:42.350
including utilizing the
Whistle transformation language

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:44.840
in order to get your
data to a common data

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:47.000
model it can persist in.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:48.950
While this
architecture highlights

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:52.080
utilizing Cloud Data
Fusion for harmonization,

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:55.790
as Marianne discussed, users can
also leverage our Google Cloud

00:19:55.790 --> 00:20:00.200
health care API or Cloud
Dataflow as the infrastructure

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:03.210
to do these transformations.

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:06.480
Once it's in a persistent layer,
whether that be in the health

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:10.770
care API under FHIR or
perhaps in BigQuery,

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:13.520
we really moved to
the enrichment stage.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:15.840
And this is where users
get to explore their data

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:18.480
and drive insights
from their data.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:22.210
So users can connect with a
plethora of other Google Cloud

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:26.830
products such as our AI
platforms or AI platform

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:31.360
notebooks, which is really just
a managed Jupyter notebook.

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:34.090
We even allow for
applications that

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:37.360
are hosted on our
marketplace for VM images

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:41.440
to be able to use some of the
other applications that'll

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:44.680
be publishing by
other institutions

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:47.370
within your data set.

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:49.590
The second architecture
that I go over

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:52.150
is really more of a
technical architecture

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:54.340
of the flow of the data.

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:58.270
As I discussed, the various
different source systems

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:01.660
result in various different
ingestion processes.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:04.450
So while batch data
or historical data

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:08.080
might come in on Cloud
Storage, real time data

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:14.410
comes in usually within HL7
v2 or perhaps device data.

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:17.380
That's going to be handled
on a transactional database

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:20.273
and may land in the health
care API store or one

00:21:20.273 --> 00:21:24.970
of our other application-based
products such as our app

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:26.220
engine.

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:28.420
The transformation
and harmonization step

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:30.940
here is happening with
Cloud Data Fusion,

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:33.100
and mean there is the
various different ways

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:35.530
that data can be handled.

00:21:35.530 --> 00:21:39.260
Metadata can appear one
way, and other data,

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:42.700
such as your analytics
data, your deidentify data,

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:45.820
can be surfaced through BigQuery
and utilized with products

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:48.590
such as our private catalog.

