00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:54.620
you have to use the safe
offset command within BigQuery.

00:29:54.620 --> 00:29:59.840
That allows you to see a flat
view of patient IDs, phones,

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:04.920
birthdays, addresses,
things like this.

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:09.800
Now, for those who don't want to
write such a complicated query

00:30:09.800 --> 00:30:14.360
against BigQuery, or maybe are
just not as familiar with SQL,

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:17.870
we had a recent
acquisition, Looker,

00:30:17.870 --> 00:30:21.020
who the semantic
layer within Looker

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:25.280
actually can understand
the FHIR data standard

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:27.590
and write the queries for you.

00:30:27.590 --> 00:30:32.150
So it can take that complicated
nests and arrays within,

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:35.870
unnest it on the fly, and
create beautiful visualizations

00:30:35.870 --> 00:30:39.110
for this complicated data model.

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:42.680
Here we have an example
of a patient that

00:30:42.680 --> 00:30:45.290
is utilizing Looker,
and their data

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:47.840
is stored in the
FHIR data standard.

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:50.440
So you can see they're
from Massachusetts.

00:30:50.440 --> 00:30:52.200
80 years old.

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:53.870
This is an encounter timeline.

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:58.730
The larger a diamond is, the
longer the encounter was.

00:30:58.730 --> 00:31:01.490
And you can kind of see
their vitals over time.

00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:03.950
This can be effective
patient management

00:31:03.950 --> 00:31:07.610
for doctors or clinicians
to be able to understand

00:31:07.610 --> 00:31:15.090
what their patient looks like
across the continuum of care.

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:18.600
We then can also go over to
more of an administrative view,

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:22.290
which is allowing for us to
visualize how many patients

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:24.180
is going through
a system, what's

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:27.360
the average length of
stay or readmission rate,

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:31.180
as well as an overview
of your population.

00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:33.640
For any of these
particular statistics,

00:31:33.640 --> 00:31:36.090
we actually have the
ability to drill into them.

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:41.480
So I have the ability
to drill into and see

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:45.230
each of the patients that
are making up that statistic.

00:31:45.230 --> 00:31:47.420
If I'm interested in
this particular patient,

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:50.030
I have the ability to
follow up with him,

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:54.120
or see his overview which is
that very similar dashboard

00:31:54.120 --> 00:31:56.510
to the last one we
were seeing for each

00:31:56.510 --> 00:31:58.910
of the individual patients.

00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:01.640
Looker going to be a
great tool to abstract

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:04.970
that complicated data
model into a layer

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:09.050
that you are able to
drive meaning from,

00:32:09.050 --> 00:32:13.160
gain insights from, as well as
even do some machine learning

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:14.990
modeling here.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:16.580
Thank you so much for coming.

00:32:16.580 --> 00:32:20.930
For more information, check out
our harmonization GitHub repo

00:32:20.930 --> 00:32:24.430
and contact your sales
rep on how to get started.

